The only change: two comments
-----------------------------
`simproc_system.sv` carried the same to-do twice, on the two lines that decide
which register ORI accumulates into:

    line 635   rf_reg_a_in     = 2'b0; // change this to 1, to match SimProc
    line 689   rf_reg_w_in     = 2'b0; // change this to 1, to match SimProc

DD104's premise is "there are deliberately hidden bugs in this file". A comment
that says *change this* is a planted-looking clue pointing at code that is not
wrong, and it would send students down a two-hour dead end on their first read.
Both comments are gone, replaced by a statement of what the instruction does.
The full argument, the four variants that were simulated to settle it, and the
open question for the RTL author are in ../../ORI-DECISION.md.

    -                    // rA <- RF[0]
    -                    rf_reg_a_in     = 2'b0; // change this to 1, to match SimProc
    +                    // ORI's accumulator is R0 (DD104 spec; see ORI-DECISION.md)
    +                    rf_reg_a_in     = 2'b0;

    -                // RF[0] <- ALU out (change later to be SimProc accurate)
    -                    rf_reg_w_in     = 2'b0; // change this to 1, to match SimProc
    +                // RF[0] <- ALU out.  ORI's accumulator is R0 (DD104 spec).
    +                    rf_reg_w_in     = 2'b0;

No logic changed and no line moved: the fork is still 1178 lines, and every
line number in the bug catalogue, the labs and the writeups means the same
thing here as it does upstream. `diff` the two files and you get exactly the
four lines above.

The half-applied version of that same edit — write R1, keep reading R0 — is a
real bug that hides behind a reset, and it ships as variant bug-11.

Files deliberately NOT copied
-----------------------------
info.yaml, src/config.json, README.md, .github/, .devcontainer/, .vscode/,
docs/info.md.

All of it is TinyTapeout shuttle machinery: tile counts, the LibreLane
hardening config, the "submit your design to the next shuttle" README, the
GDS/FPGA/docs CI. Decision D5 puts the shuttle out of scope — SimProc is
DD104's RTL vehicle, not a tapeout — so none of it ships and DD104 never
teaches submission.

What does stay is `src/tt_um_ieeeuoftasic_simproc.sv`, because DD104 drives the
chip through its real pins, and that file is the pin map:

    ui_in[0]  = uart_rx           uo_out[0] = uart_tx
                                  uo_out[1] = halt
    uio_in[7:0] -> clk_per_bit    uo_out[2] = done
      = uio_in x 4                rst_n is active LOW

And `test/` stays, verbatim, because it is the course's opening argument. The
only test this chip has ever had asserts `uo_out == 3` after 100 clocks — UART
idle high, halt high, done low. It asserts that the processor is *not running*.
It sends no command, loads no program, and executes no instruction. It passes on
all thirteen bugs in variants/.

Keeping this fork current
-------------------------
Every bug patch is anchored to exact source text, not just a line number
(verify/bugs.py). If upstream changes a line a bug sits on, re-running

    python3 verify/make_patches.py

fails loudly and names the line, instead of quietly producing a different bug.
That is the intended way to find out that a merge broke the catalogue.
